ecomposition would force both summands to have large intersections with qua-\n\ndratic images. Our proof combines a weighted entropy argument with sieve estimates, inspired by\n\nthe recent work of Croot–Mao–Pohoata–Sheffer–Yip.\n\n1. I NTRODUCTION\n\nLet N be a positive integer and let A⊆[N]. For each prime p, write\n\nA p={a(mod p):a∈A}\n\nfor the set of residue classes occupied by A modulo p. The large sieve [14] and Gallagher’s larger\n\nsieve [7] turn upper bounds for the sets A p into global upper bounds for |A|. For background on\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nINVERSE QUESTIONS FOR THE LARGE SIEVE 3 (https://wrap.warwick.ac.uk/id/eprint/107458/7/WRAP-inverse-questions-large-sieve-Green-2018.pdf)\nciteturn8search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.6 years ago; Crawled: 8.2 years ago; After proving the foregoing results, we turn to the “robustness” of the inverse large sieve problem. ... Together with Theorem 1.4, this provides some motivation for making the following conjecture of the form “almost equality in the large sieve implies quadratic structure”. ... **Theorem 1.6.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n100 OPEN PROBLEMS                                                                 29 (https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/greenbj/papers/open-problems.pdf)\nciteturn8search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; ADDITIVE AND COMBINATORIAL NUMBER THEORY ... Adam Harper and me [154] showed that a positive solution follows from a reasonable understanding of the *inverse large sieve*, which is Problem 47 above. ... ** This was asked by Erdős, several times I think, but certainly in a collection [110] from 1981 one finds the following problem, which is essentially equivalent: ‘An old conjecture of mine states that if \\(f(n)\\) is the least integer not of the form \\(a+b\\) with \\(P(a,b)\\le n\\) then for every \\(k\\) and for \\(n>n_0(k)\\) we have \\(f(n)>n^k\